WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.720
Okay, so what is exactly distinct?

00:05.720 --> 00:09.520
If you use it in SQL, it's going to go and remove duplicates in your data.

00:09.560 --> 00:15.480
Duplicates are like repeated values in your data, and it can make sure that each value appears only

00:15.480 --> 00:16.840
once in the results.

00:16.840 --> 00:18.040
So it sounds very simple.

00:18.040 --> 00:19.760
And as well the syntax is easy.

00:19.760 --> 00:22.480
So as usual we start always with a select by.

00:22.520 --> 00:27.440
Directly after the select we use the keyword distinct, so there is nothing between them.

00:27.440 --> 00:28.600
And then the normal stuff.

00:28.600 --> 00:32.600
We specify the columns and then the from in order to get the data from table.

00:32.600 --> 00:35.760
Let's say that I would like to get a list of unique values of the country.

00:35.760 --> 00:40.600
So the first thing that SQL gonna do, of course, is to get the data from the database using the from.

00:40.600 --> 00:43.080
And now the second step is the select.

00:43.080 --> 00:44.360
So SQL can execute it.

00:44.360 --> 00:50.080
And going to select only one column the country all other columns can be excluded or removed from the

00:50.080 --> 00:50.560
results.

00:50.600 --> 00:52.360
And now SQL can go to the third step.

00:52.360 --> 00:55.760
It's going to go and apply the Distincts on the country values.

00:55.760 --> 01:00.520
So it acts like a filter where it's going to make sure each value happens only once.

01:00.520 --> 01:02.400
So it's going to start with the first value Germany.

01:02.440 --> 01:03.800
Now it's going to look to the results.

01:03.800 --> 01:04.640
Do we have Germany.

01:04.640 --> 01:08.690
Well we don't have anything yet so that's why it's going to include it in the results.

01:08.690 --> 01:10.330
Then the next value is going to be USA.

01:10.490 --> 01:11.050
The same thing.

01:11.050 --> 01:14.490
We don't have USA in the results so it can go and include it.

01:14.490 --> 01:15.810
And this happens as well.

01:15.810 --> 01:19.850
For the UK, we don't have UK in the final results, that's why it's going to go as well.

01:19.850 --> 01:20.490
Included.

01:20.490 --> 01:22.210
Now comes Germany again.

01:22.210 --> 01:27.610
Now it's going to say wait, we have it already so it will not go and add it again in the outputs because

01:27.610 --> 01:29.330
it must appear only once.

01:29.330 --> 01:34.490
So we will not have Germany twice and as well for the last value, the USA, we have it already in the

01:34.490 --> 01:35.010
results.

01:35.010 --> 01:37.130
That's why it will not appear again.

01:37.130 --> 01:41.530
And with that we have removed the duplicates or the repetition inside our data.

01:41.530 --> 01:42.970
So each value is unique.

01:42.970 --> 01:44.850
Now let's go back to SQL okay.

01:44.890 --> 01:46.010
That task is very simple.

01:46.010 --> 01:49.170
It says return unique list of all countries.

01:49.170 --> 01:50.770
So let's go and do that.

01:50.810 --> 01:51.730
It's going to be funny.

01:51.730 --> 01:52.450
So select.

01:52.490 --> 01:55.130
And now let's get the column country from our table.

01:55.170 --> 01:56.690
Customers like this.

01:56.690 --> 01:58.810
Now you can see we have a list of all countries.

01:58.810 --> 02:01.170
But the task says we need a unique list.

02:01.170 --> 02:03.650
So that means I cannot have repetitions inside it.

02:03.650 --> 02:06.370
And with that you can use the very nice distinct.

02:06.770 --> 02:10.490
So if you do it like this let's go and execute.

02:10.490 --> 02:12.810
You will see there will be no duplicates in your results.

02:12.810 --> 02:14.980
And all the values in the result can be unique.

02:14.980 --> 02:16.420
So thus we have solved the task.

02:16.460 --> 02:17.540
It's it's very simple.

02:17.580 --> 02:22.900
Now, there is one thing about the distincts that I see a lot of people using it, a lot in cases that

02:22.900 --> 02:24.260
it's not really necessary.

02:24.340 --> 02:26.940
So for example, let's go and get the ID.

02:26.980 --> 02:31.780
Now if you go and execute it, you can see here we have a list of all IDs and there are no duplicates.

02:31.780 --> 02:36.820
But now if I go and remove that distinct and execute it, we will get the same results.

02:36.820 --> 02:38.860
Because the IDs are usually unique.

02:38.860 --> 02:43.860
So it really makes no sense to go and say distincts because as you can see, the database has to go

02:43.860 --> 02:46.340
and make sure each value happens only once.

02:46.340 --> 02:50.980
So there is like extra work for the SQL and it is usually an expensive operation.

02:50.980 --> 02:56.700
So if your data is already unique, don't go and apply Distincts only if you see repetitions and duplicates,

02:56.700 --> 03:00.540
and you don't want to see that only in this scenario go and apply that distincts.

03:00.540 --> 03:05.460
Don't go blindly for each query applying distinct just in case there is duplicates.

03:05.460 --> 03:07.060
This is usually bad practices.

03:07.100 --> 03:08.900
Okay, so that's all for Distincts.

03:08.940 --> 03:09.700
Okay my friends.

03:09.700 --> 03:13.420
So with that you have learned how to remove the duplicates using the Distincts.

03:13.420 --> 03:18.100
In the next step, we're going to talk about another keyword that you can use together with the selects.

03:18.100 --> 03:20.620
You can use top in order to limit your data.

03:20.620 --> 03:22.580
So now let's go and understand what this means.
